      "page_idx": 13,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.046,
            0.364,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.088,
            0.443,
            0.112
          ],
          "content": "**Order of Multiplication**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.196,
            0.386,
            0.234
          ],
          "content": "We know that ${ \\frac { 1 } { 2 } } \\times { \\frac { 1 } { 4 } } = { \\frac { 1 } { 8 } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.634,
            0.126,
            0.843,
            0.298
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.152,
            0.302,
            0.364,
            0.472
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_13_image_4.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.394,
            0.414,
            0.596,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "Now, what is $\\frac{1}{4} \\times \\frac{1}{2} ;$ That is $\\frac{1}{8}$ too."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.545,
            0.848,
            0.583
          ],
          "content": "In general, note that the area of a rectangle remains the same even if the length and breadth are interchanged."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.582,
            0.672,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": "The order of multiplication does not matter. Thus,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.432,
            0.616,
            0.563,
            0.652
          ],
          "content": "\\frac{a}{b} \\times \\frac{c}{d} = \\frac{c}{d} \\times \\frac{a}{b}.",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_8.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.663,
            0.848,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "This can also be seen from Brahmagupta’s formula for multiplying fractions."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.725,
            0.495,
            0.749
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "8.2 Division of Fractions"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.758,
            0.537,
            0.815
          ],
          "content": "What is $12 \\div 4?$ You know this already. But can this problem be restated as a multiplication problem?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.814,
            0.536,
            0.853
          ],
          "content": "What should be multiplied by 4 to get 12? That is,"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.294,
            0.862,
            0.393,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": "4 \\times ? = 1 2",
          "image_source": "images/page_13_equation_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.6,
            0.752,
            0.845,
            0.84
          ],
